==============================================================
6. The tail that appeared when the median got seven times better
==============================================================
machine   : Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8124M CPU @ 3.00GHz
topology  : 4 physical cores x 2 threads
affinity  : servers 2,6,3,7, generator 1,5
reserved  : 0,4 left to the kernel -- see lib.sh for why
load      : wrk -t4 -c100 -d10s, 5 reps, median
date      : 2026-08-15 19:23 UTC

On /users/42 HEAD serves 3.80x the throughput of the baseline and
halves p50 seven times over -- while p99 goes from 99ms to 307ms and
the spread from 0.9% to 12.2%.

The hypothesis: HEAD is now fast enough that the CONNECTION POOL is
the queue. 2 processes x pool 10 is 60 database connections
against 100 client connections, so 40 requests are always waiting for
a slot. The baseline was slow enough per request that the client never
piled up like that.

If that is right, the tail collapses when the connections fit -- and
does not when the pool is merely bigger than the demand.

  configuration                 req/s        p50        p99   spread
  pool 10  conns 50          7338.34     5.52ms    22.57ms     2.7%
  pool 10  conns 100         7044.75     5.33ms      4.88s     1.1%
  pool 30  conns 100         7715.17    11.90ms    23.94ms     4.9%
  pool 30  conns 200         7050.82    25.93ms    80.93ms     3.3%

  (pool x 2 processes is the real connection count: 10 -> 60, 30 -> 180)

==============================================================
7. What the per-request deadline costs in the tail
==============================================================
machine   : Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8124M CPU @ 3.00GHz
topology  : 4 physical cores x 2 threads
affinity  : servers 2,6,3,7, generator 1,5
reserved  : 0,4 left to the kernel -- see lib.sh for why
load      : wrk -t4 -c100 -d10s, 5 reps, median
date      : 2026-08-15 19:27 UTC

In part 2, akkar showed p99 647ms on /users/42 and akkar-lean showed
86ms on the same route and the same load. The only difference between
them is timeout = 0, which bypasses the deadline controller.

Those were two separate runs, so that is a suspicion and not a result.
This is the controlled version: same process, alternating, five
repetitions, at the concurrency where part 6 showed the pool queues.

  variant               req/s        p50          p99   spread
  shipped             7539.71    12.19ms      27.40ms     3.2%
  lean                8596.92    10.48ms      27.55ms     1.1%

  shipped = the deadline as akkar ships it; lean = timeout 0, no controller

